This study investigates solutions to elliptic equations featuring the regional fractional Laplacian, defined as:
\begin{equation}\label{0}
  \arraycolsep=1pt \begin{array}{lll}
  \displaystyle (-\Delta)^\alpha_\Omega u=f \qquad & {\rm in}\quad
\Omega,\\[2mm] \phantom{ (-\Delta)^\alpha }
  \displaystyle u=g\quad & {\rm on}\quad \partial \Omega, \end{array}
\end{equation}
where $\Omega$ represents a bounded open subset of $\mathbb{R}^N$ ($N\ge 2$) possessing a $C^2$ boundary $\partial\Omega$, $\alpha$ lies in the interval $(\frac12,1)$, and $(-\Delta)^\alpha_\Omega$ signifies the regional fractional Laplacian operator. We establish that if $g\equiv0$, problem (\ref{0}) possesses a unique weak solution under the conditions that $f$ belongs to $L^2(\Omega)$, $L^1(\Omega, \rho^\beta dx)$, or $\mathcal{M}(\Omega,\rho^\beta)$. In this context, $\rho(x)$ denotes the distance from $x$ to $\partial\Omega$, $\beta$ is defined as $2\alpha-1$, and $\mathcal{M}(\Omega,\rho^\beta)$ comprises all Radon measures $\nu$ for which $\int_\Omega \rho^\beta d|\nu|<+\infty$. Furthermore, we derive an Integral by Parts Formula applicable to the classical solution of (\ref{0}) when general boundary data $g$ is present.

This study examines the Dirichlet problem \begin{equation} \label{iniz}
\begin{cases} -\Delta u = \rho^2 (e^{u} - e^{-u}) & \text{ in } \Omega\\ u=0 &
\text{ on } \partial \Omega, \end{cases} \end{equation} where $\Omega$ is a bounded $C^2$ domain in $\mathbb{R}^2$ and $\rho$ is a small parameter. While reference [1] establishes the existence of an $m$-point blow-up solution $u_\rho$ along with its asymptotic behavior, we determine the Morse index of $u_\rho$ by relating it to the Morse index of the associated Hamilton function. Furthermore, we provide an asymptotic estimate for the first $4m$ eigenvalues and their corresponding eigenfunctions.

Let $\mathbb{K}$ denote a complete non-Archimedean valued field that satisfies the condition $\left|\sum_{j=1}^{n}\lambda_j^2\right|=\max_{1\leq j \leq n}|\lambda_j|^2$ for all $\lambda_j \in \mathbb{K}$ and $n \in \mathbb{N}$. For a given $d \in \mathbb{N}$, we consider $\mathbb{K}^d$ as the standard $d$-dimensional non-Archimedean Hilbert space and $\text{Sym}^m(\mathbb{K}^d)$ as the associated Hilbert space of symmetric $m$-tensors. The main result states that if a set $\{\tau_j\}_{j=1}^n$ in $\mathbb{K}^d$ consists of unit vectors ($\langle \tau_j, \tau_j\rangle =1$) and the operator defined by $x \mapsto \sum_{j=1}^n\langle x, \tau_j^{\otimes m}\rangle \tau_j^{\otimes m}$ on $\text{Sym}^m(\mathbb{K}^d)$ is diagonalizable, then the following inequality holds:
\begin{align} (1) \quad \quad \quad \max_{1\leq j,k \leq n, j \neq k}\{|n|, |\langle \tau_j, \tau_k\rangle|^{2m} \}\geq \frac{|n|^2}{\left|{d+m-1 \choose m}\right| }. \end{align}
This inequality represents the non-Archimedean analogue of Welch bounds, originally established by Welch in *IEEE Transactions on Information Theory* (1974). Furthermore, we formulate the non-Archimedean version of the Zauner conjecture.

This study presents lifetime measurements for the $\Lambda_c^+$, $\Xi_c^+$, and $\Xi_c^0$ charm baryons based on proton-proton collision data at center-of-mass energies of 7 and 8\tev, utilizing an integrated luminosity of 3.0 fb$^{-1}$ collected by the LHCb experiment. These charm baryons are identified via the decay channels $\Lambda_c^+\to pK^-\pi^+$, $\Xi_c^+\to pK^-\pi^+$, and $\Xi_c^0\to pK^-K^-\pi^+$, and they stem from the semimuonic decays of beauty baryons. The lifetimes are measured relative to the $D^+$ meson, yielding the following results:
\begin{align*}
  \tau_{\Lambda_c^+} &= 203.5\pm1.0\pm1.3\pm1.4~{\rm fs}, \newline
  \tau_{\Xi_c^+} &= 456.8\pm3.5\pm2.9\pm3.1~{\rm fs}, \newline
  \tau_{\Xi_c^0} &= 154.5\pm1.7\pm1.6\pm1.0~{\rm fs}, \end{align*}
where the uncertainties correspond to statistical, systematic, and $D^+$ lifetime uncertainty contributions, respectively. These findings are roughly 3 to 4 times more precise than existing world average values. While the $\Lambda_c^+$ and $\Xi_c^+$ lifetimes align with previous measurements, the $\Xi_c^0$ lifetime exceeds the world average by approximately 3.3 standard deviations.

