Braverman, Finkelberg, and Nakajima defined the generalized affine Grassmannian slices $\overline{\mathcal W}^{\lambda}_{\mu}$ and demonstrated their identification as Coulomb branches of $3d$ $\mathcal N=4$ gauge theories. We establish their conjecture that a transversal slice of $\mathcal W^{
u}_{\mu}$ within $\overline{\mathcal W}^{\lambda}_{\mu}$ is isomorphic to $\overline{\mathcal W}^{\lambda}_{
u}$. Furthermore, addressing their hypothesis that Coulomb branches of $3d$ $\mathcal N=4$ gauge theories possess symplectic singularities, we verify this property for the generalized affine Grassmannian slices $\overline{\mathcal W}^{\lambda}_{\mu}$. In the process, we provide an alternative proof for the smoothness of $\mathcal W^{
u}_{\mu}$, a result originally established by Muthiah and Weekes via a distinct approach.

This paper investigates the nonexistence of positive supersolutions for a Lane-Emden system featuring inverse-square potentials, defined as:
\begin{equation}\label{0}
  \left\{ \begin{array}{lll}
  -\Delta u+\frac{\mu_1}{|x|^2} u= v^p \quad {\rm in}\ \,
\Omega\setminus\{0\},\\[2mm]
  -\Delta v+\frac{\mu_2}{|x|^2} v= u^q \quad {\rm in}\ \, \Omega\setminus\{0\}
  \end{array}
  \right.
\end{equation}
where $p,q>0$, $\mu_1,\mu_2\geq -(N-2)^2/4$, $\Omega$ is a smooth bounded domain in $\mathbb{R}^N$ ($N\geq 3$) containing the origin, and the parameters satisfy either $-(N-2)^2/4\leq \mu_1,\mu_2<0$ or $-(N-2)^2/4\leq \mu_1<0\leq \mu_2$. We establish sharp supercritical regions for $(p,q)$ regarding the nonexistence of positive supersolutions for system (\ref{0}). The presence of negative coefficients $\mu_1,\mu_2$ in the inverse-square potentials implies an initial singularity at the origin; consequently, in the supercritical regime, an iterative procedure is employed to enhance the blow-up rate until the nonlinear terms become inadmissible within certain weighted $L^1$ spaces. Conversely, in the subcritical case, we demonstrate the existence of positive supersolutions for system (\ref{s 1.1}) utilizing specific radially symmetric functions.
